# Malaria Parasite Detection Using CNN and Transfer Learning

## 📌 Overview

This project uses Deep Learning to classify blood smear cell images into two classes:

- **Parasitized**
- **Uninfected**

Two models are implemented:
1. CNN trained from scratch
2. MobileNetV2 using Transfer Learning and Fine-Tuning

The project is based on the research paper by Rajaraman et al. (2018).

## 🎯 Objectives

- Detect malaria parasites from cell images.
- Implement a CNN from scratch.
- Apply Transfer Learning using MobileNetV2.
- Compare model performance using Accuracy, Precision, Recall and F1-score.
- Compare the results with the research paper.

## 📊 Dataset

**NIH/NLM Malaria Cell Images Dataset**

- Total dataset: 27,558 images
- Parasitized: 13,779
- Uninfected: 13,779
- This project uses a balanced subset of **4,000 images**.

**Dataset:**  
https://data.lhncbc.nlm.nih.gov/public/Malaria/cell_images.zip

## ⚙️ Methodology

```text
Dataset
   ↓
Preprocessing
   ↓
Image Resizing (128×128)
   ↓
Normalization + Augmentation
   ↓
70% Training / 15% Validation / 15% Testing
   ↓
CNN From Scratch
       +
MobileNetV2 Transfer Learning
   ↓
Fine-Tuning
   ↓
Performance Evaluation

🧠 Models
CNN From Scratch

A custom CNN learns image features directly from the malaria dataset.

MobileNetV2

A pretrained MobileNetV2 model is used with ImageNet weights. The model is first used for transfer learning and then fine-tuned for malaria classification.

📈 Results
Model	Accuracy	Precision	Recall	F1-Score
CNN From Scratch	95.83%	95.08%	96.67%	95.87%
MobileNetV2	84.50%	77.45%	97.33%	86.26%
Result

The CNN From Scratch achieved the best overall performance with 95.83% accuracy and 95.87% F1-score.

MobileNetV2 achieved a higher recall of 97.33%, meaning it detected more positive cases in the test set.

📚 Research Paper

Rajaraman et al. (2018)
Pre-trained convolutional neural networks as feature extractors toward improved malaria parasite detection in thin blood smear images.

DOI:
https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj.4568

The research paper evaluated pretrained CNN architectures for malaria parasite detection. Our implementation is inspired by the paper but is not an exact reproduction, as we use a 4,000-image subset, MobileNetV2, and a different data-splitting strategy.

